{
  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: The best multimodal result (config_006, scgpt+vit_l, avgpool, weighted: acc 0.5420, F1 0.4833) is only marginally better than RNA-only multi-profile scgpt (config_002: acc 0.5305, F1 0.5152), and notably has a lower F1. Multimodal with resnet50 (config_034: 0.5251) is actually worse than RNA-only alone. Image-only with resnet50 (config_042: 0.3275) is far below RNA-only, but we lack the image-only baseline with the stronger vit_l encoder. Uncertainty: We cannot yet determine whether the small multimodal gain over RNA-only is genuinely attributable to the image modality or is within noise, because we don't know how well vit_l performs on images alone in the multi-profile setting. Question: config_004 (image_only, multiprofile, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool) isolates the image encoder's standalone contribution with the stronger backbone, directly comparable to config_042 (resnet50) and config_006 (multimodal vit_l). Why best next: This single-variable experiment (changing only the image encoder from resnet50 to vit_l in the image-only setting) is essential to calibrate whether the image modality is adding real signal in the multimodal configs. If vit_l alone approaches ~0.50, the multimodal gain is minimal and we should refocus on RNA encoder/fusion improvements; if it stays near 0.33, the vit_l contribution in multimodal is more meaningful. This reduces uncertainty about the most fundamental design decision (whether to invest in multimodal fusion at all) before spending further budget on fusion or aggregation variants."
}

